+# Chromium Experimental Ray Query
+
+The `chromium_experimental_ray_query` extension adds inline hardware ray tracing (ray queries) to
+WGSL. A shader in any stage can trace a ray against an acceleration structure, step through the
+candidate intersections found during traversal, and read back the closest committed intersection.
+
+The feature is modeled on Vulkan's [`SPV_KHR_ray_query`](https://github.khronos.org/SPIRV-Registry/extensions/KHR/SPV_KHR_ray_query.html)
+/ `GL_EXT_ray_query`. It also corresponds closely to DXR 1.1 `RayQuery` and Metal's
+`intersection_query`.
+
+## Status
+
+Ray query support in Tint is experimental and might change as we gain implementation experience.
+It will be updated to follow an upstream specification if one is made. Specification work in the
+WebGPU group hasn't started.
+
+Only the SPIR-V backend implements the extension. The HLSL, MSL and GLSL backends reject any module
+that uses it, with errors such as
+`ray queries (chromium_experimental_ray_query) are not supported by the HLSL backend`.
+The WGSL writer, the inspector and the IR binary format all support it.
+
+## Pseudo-specification
+
+All the types and builtin functions below need the extension to be enabled:
+
+```wgsl
+enable chromium_experimental_ray_query;
+```
+
+Using any of them without the `enable` directive is an error, for example
+`use of 'ray_query' requires enabling extension 'chromium_experimental_ray_query'`.
+
+### `acceleration_structure`
+
+`acceleration_structure` is an opaque handle type for a top-level acceleration structure.
+
+* It can only be the type of a module-scope `var` that has no address space and has `@group` and
+  `@binding` attributes, like a texture or sampler.
+* It can be passed by value to builtin functions and as a parameter of user-declared functions.
+* It is not constructible. It cannot be the type of a `let`, `const` or `override`, a function
+  return type, an array element, a structure member, or a `binding_array` element.
+
+### `ray_query`
+
+`ray_query` is an opaque object that holds the state of a single ray traversal.
+
+* It can only be the store type of a `var` in the `function` or `private` address space. The
+  `var` cannot have an initializer.
+* A `ray_query` cannot be loaded, copied, assigned or used as a value. It cannot be the type of a
+  `let`, `const`, `override`, function value parameter or function return type. It cannot be
+  constructed or dereferenced, and it cannot be an array element or a structure member.
+* It is accessed through pointers: `&rq` is passed to the builtins below. User-declared functions
+  can take `ptr<function, ray_query>` and `ptr<private, ray_query>` parameters.
+* A ray query starts out uninitialized. A `function`-scope `var` is uninitialized each time its
+  declaration runs (for example, on each iteration of a loop). A `private` `var` is uninitialized
+  at the start of each invocation.
+
+### `RayDesc`
+
+`RayDesc` is a predeclared structure that describes a ray:
+
+```wgsl
+struct RayDesc {
+  flags : u32,         // Ray flags, see below
+  cull_mask : u32,     // Instances whose mask ANDed with cull_mask is zero are skipped (8 LSBs)
+  t_min : f32,         // Start of the ray interval
+  t_max : f32,         // End of the ray interval
+  origin : vec3<f32>,  // World-space ray origin
+  dir : vec3<f32>,     // World-space ray direction
+}
+```
+
+`RayDesc` is constructible and host-shareable (size 48, align 16), so it can be used in storage and
+uniform buffers. It has the usual zero-value (`RayDesc()`) and member-wise constructors.
+
+### `RayIntersection`
+
+`RayIntersection` is a predeclared structure that describes a committed or candidate intersection:
+
+```wgsl
+struct RayIntersection {
+  kind : u32,                     // See the table below
+  t : f32,                        // Distance along the ray
+  instance_custom_data : u32,     // 24-bit instanceCustomIndex of the instance
+  instance_index : u32,           // Index of the instance in the top-level structure
+  sbt_record_offset : u32,        // instanceShaderBindingTableRecordOffset of the instance
+  geometry_index : u32,           // Index of the geometry in the bottom-level structure
+  primitive_index : u32,          // Index of the primitive in the geometry
+  barycentrics : vec2<f32>,       // Triangle barycentrics, (u, v) of vertices 1 and 2
+  front_face : bool,              // True if the ray hit the front face of a triangle
+  object_to_world : mat4x3<f32>,  // Object to world transform of the instance
+  world_to_object : mat4x3<f32>,  // World to object transform of the instance
+}
+```
+
+`RayIntersection` is constructible but contains a `bool`, so it is not host-shareable.
+
+| `kind` | Meaning                                                        | Reported by          |
+|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
+| 0      | No intersection                                                | committed, candidate |
+| 1      | Triangle                                                       | committed, candidate |
+| 2      | Generated (procedural, committed by `rayQueryGenerateIntersection`) | committed only  |
+| 3      | Axis-aligned bounding box (procedural primitive)               | candidate only       |
+
+When `kind` is 0 every member is zero or `false`. `barycentrics` and `front_face` are only set for
+triangles. For `kind == 3` candidates, `t` is 0, because the distance to a procedural primitive is
+only known to the shader.
+
+Like other predeclared types, `RayDesc` and `RayIntersection` can be shadowed by user
+declarations.
+
+### Ray flags
+
+Ray flag constants are not predeclared. The values match the SPIR-V `RayFlags` mask:
+
+| Flag                       | Value   |
+|----------------------------|---------|
+| `Opaque`                   | `0x001` |
+| `NoOpaque`                 | `0x002` |
+| `TerminateOnFirstHit`      | `0x004` |
+| `SkipClosestHitShader`     | `0x008` (no effect on ray queries) |
+| `CullBackFacingTriangles`  | `0x010` |
+| `CullFrontFacingTriangles` | `0x020` |
+| `CullOpaque`               | `0x040` |
+| `CullNoOpaque`             | `0x080` |
+| `SkipTriangles`            | `0x100` |
+| `SkipAABBs`                | `0x200` |
+
+Bits above `0x3FF` are ignored. A ray whose flags combine mutually exclusive bits is invalid (see
+[Defined behavior](#defined-behavior)).
+
+### Builtin functions
+
+In the table below `AS` is `function` or `private`, and `rq` has type
+`ptr<AS, ray_query, read_write>`.
+
+| Builtin | Description |
+|---------|-------------|
+| `fn rayQueryInitialize(rq, acceleration_structure : acceleration_structure, desc : RayDesc)` | Starts a new traversal of `acceleration_structure` with the ray `desc`. Any previous traversal state of `rq` is discarded. |
+| `@must_use fn rayQueryProceed(rq) -> bool` | Continues the traversal. Returns `true` if traversal stopped at a candidate intersection that the shader must evaluate (a non-opaque triangle or an AABB). Returns `false` once traversal is complete. |
+| `fn rayQueryGenerateIntersection(rq, hit_t : f32)` | Commits the current AABB candidate as a generated intersection at distance `hit_t`. |
+| `fn rayQueryConfirmIntersection(rq)` | Commits the current triangle candidate. |
+| `fn rayQueryTerminate(rq)` | Ends the traversal. The next `rayQueryProceed` returns `false`. |
+| `@must_use fn rayQueryGetCommittedIntersection(rq) -> RayIntersection` | Returns the closest intersection committed so far. |
+| `@must_use fn rayQueryGetCandidateIntersection(rq) -> RayIntersection` | Returns the current candidate intersection. |
+
+### Defined behavior
+
+Many uses of the SPIR-V ray query instructions are undefined behavior. WGSL has no undefined
+behavior, so Tint tracks the state of each ray query and guards every builtin call:
+
+* `rayQueryInitialize` checks the ray. A ray is invalid if any component of `origin` or `dir` is
+  not finite, `t_min` is negative or NaN, `t_max` is less than `t_min` or NaN, or `flags` has more
+  than one bit set in any of these groups:
+  * `SkipTriangles` and `SkipAABBs`
+  * `SkipTriangles`, `CullBackFacingTriangles` and `CullFrontFacingTriangles`
+  * `Opaque`, `NoOpaque`, `CullOpaque` and `CullNoOpaque`
+
+  An invalid ray leaves the ray query uninitialized. It then behaves as if it had found no
+  intersections.
+* Before `rayQueryInitialize` has been called, `rayQueryProceed` returns `false` and the other
+  builtins do nothing or return a zero `RayIntersection`.
+* Once traversal is complete (`rayQueryProceed` returned `false`, or `rayQueryTerminate` was
+  called), `rayQueryProceed` keeps returning `false`.
+* `rayQueryGenerateIntersection` does nothing unless the last `rayQueryProceed` returned `true`,
+  the candidate is an AABB, and `t_min <= hit_t <= t_committed`. `t_committed` is the `t` of the
+  committed intersection, or the ray's `t_max` if there isn't one. A NaN `hit_t` does nothing.
+* `rayQueryConfirmIntersection` does nothing unless the last `rayQueryProceed` returned `true`
+  and the candidate is a triangle.
+* `rayQueryTerminate` only has an effect if the ray query is initialized and traversal is not
+  complete.
+* `rayQueryGetCandidateIntersection` returns a zero `RayIntersection` (`kind == 0`) unless the
+  last `rayQueryProceed` returned `true`.
+
+### Uniformity
+
+The ray query builtins have no uniformity requirements. Their results are always non-uniform: for
+example, a `workgroupBarrier()` that depends on the result of `rayQueryProceed` is a uniformity
+error.
+
+## Example usage
+
+```wgsl
+enable chromium_experimental_ray_query;
+
+@group(0) @binding(0) var tlas : acceleration_structure;
+@group(0) @binding(1) var<storage, read_write> output : array<vec4f>;
+
+// Returns the distance to the procedural primitive of the candidate intersection `c`.
+fn hit_sphere(c : RayIntersection) -> f32 {
+  // ... Intersect the ray with a sphere, using c.primitive_index and c.world_to_object ...
+  return 1.0;
+}
+
+@compute @workgroup_size(64)
+fn main(@builtin(global_invocation_id) id : vec3u) {
+  let desc = RayDesc(0x0u, 0xFFu, 0.001, 1000.0,
+                     vec3f(f32(id.x), 0.0, -10.0), vec3f(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));
+
+  var rq : ray_query;
+  rayQueryInitialize(&rq, tlas, desc);
+  while (rayQueryProceed(&rq)) {
+    let candidate = rayQueryGetCandidateIntersection(&rq);
+    if (candidate.kind == 3u) {
+      // Procedural AABB: compute the hit distance ourselves.
+      rayQueryGenerateIntersection(&rq, hit_sphere(candidate));
+    } else {
+      // Non-opaque triangle: accept it, e.g. after an alpha test.
+      rayQueryConfirmIntersection(&rq);
+    }
+  }
+
+  let hit = rayQueryGetCommittedIntersection(&rq);
+  if (hit.kind != 0u) {
+    output[id.x] = vec4f(hit.barycentrics, hit.t, f32(hit.primitive_index));
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Shadow rays usually only need `TerminateOnFirstHit | Opaque` and a single `rayQueryProceed` call:
+
+```wgsl
+fn occluded(accel : acceleration_structure, p : vec3f, l : vec3f) -> bool {
+  var rq : ray_query;
+  rayQueryInitialize(&rq, accel, RayDesc(0x5u, 0xFFu, 0.001, 1e30, p, l));
+  _ = rayQueryProceed(&rq);
+  return rayQueryGetCommittedIntersection(&rq).kind != 0u;
+}
+```
+
+## SPIR-V backend
+
+* `acceleration_structure` maps to `OpTypeAccelerationStructureKHR`. A module-scope variable of this
+  type is a `UniformConstant` variable decorated with `DescriptorSet` and `Binding`.
+* `ray_query` maps to `OpTypeRayQueryKHR` in the `Function` or `Private` storage class.
+* Any module that uses ray queries declares `OpExtension "SPV_KHR_ray_query"`,
+  `OpCapability RayQueryKHR` and `OpCapability RayTraversalPrimitiveCullingKHR`. The second
+  capability is needed because the `SkipTriangles` and `SkipAABBs` flags can be set at runtime.
+* The builtins map to `OpRayQueryInitializeKHR`, `OpRayQueryProceedKHR`,
+  `OpRayQueryGenerateIntersectionKHR`, `OpRayQueryConfirmIntersectionKHR`,
+  `OpRayQueryTerminateKHR`, `OpRayQueryGetRayTMinKHR`, `OpRayQueryGetIntersectionTypeKHR` and the
+  `OpRayQueryGetIntersection*KHR` getters for each member of `RayIntersection`.
+* SPIR-V 1.3 (with the extension) and later are supported, including the Vulkan memory model.
+* The `spirv.RayQuery` raise transform (`src/tint/lang/spirv/writer/raise/ray_query.cc`) does the
+  [defined behavior](#defined-behavior) guarding. For each ray query `var` it adds a hidden
+  `tint_ray_query_state` variable in the same address space: a `u32` state (uninitialized,
+  initialized, candidate or done) and the ray's `t_max`. User-declared functions with ray query
+  pointer parameters get a matching state pointer parameter. The guards cost a few ALU ops and
+  branches per builtin call.
+
+The guards cover these Vulkan valid usage rules, so the shader can't break them:
+`VUID-RuntimeSpirv-OpRayQueryInitializeKHR-06348` to `-06351` (finite origin and direction,
+non-negative and ordered `t_min`/`t_max`, no NaNs), `-06889`, `-06890` and `-06891` (exclusive ray
+flags) and `VUID-RuntimeSpirv-OpRayQueryGenerateIntersectionKHR-06353` (`hit_t` range). They also
+cover the undefined behavior rules in the `SPV_KHR_ray_query` specification: calls before
+initialization, after traversal is complete, or with the wrong candidate type.
+
+### Requirements for the Vulkan backend (Dawn)
+
+* The device must enable `VK_KHR_acceleration_structure` and `VK_KHR_ray_query`, and
+  `VkPhysicalDeviceRayQueryFeaturesKHR::rayQuery`. That feature also provides the
+  `RayTraversalPrimitiveCullingKHR` capability.
+* The extension must only be allowed (`wgsl::AllowedFeatures`) when the WebGPU feature is enabled.
+* The inspector reports `acceleration_structure` bindings with
+  `ResourceBinding::ResourceType::kAccelerationStructure`. They must be bound with
+  `VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_ACCELERATION_STRUCTURE_KHR` descriptors, and their remapping is passed in
+  `tint::Bindings::acceleration_structure`.
+* Dawn must make sure the bound acceleration structure is a top-level acceleration structure
+  (`VUID-RuntimeSpirv-OpRayQueryInitializeKHR-06352`) that was not built with motion
+  (`-06354`). The shader can't check these.
+
+## To-dos
+
+* Implement the HLSL (DXR 1.1 `RayQuery`), MSL (`intersection_query`) and GLSL (`GL_EXT_ray_query`)
+  backends.
+* Consider predeclared constants for the ray flags and the `kind` values.
+* Consider exposing more state: the candidate AABB opacity (`OpRayQueryGetIntersectionCandidateAABBOpaqueKHR`),
+  the object-space ray origin and direction, and triangle vertex positions
+  (`SPV_KHR_ray_tracing_position_fetch`).
+* Consider allowing `acceleration_structure` in `binding_array` and as the type of a `let`.
+* Ray queries are not supported by the SPIR-V reader.
